repository-code: "https://github.com/Japskua/paper_llm_for_secure_inclusive_auth"
abstract: >-
  Complete experimental dataset, generation pipeline and replication materials
  for evaluating whether asking a large language model to make authentication
  code accessible to users with cognitive conditions degrades the security of
  that code. Two user stories (password recovery for a health portal with ADHD;
  multi-factor authentication enrolment for online banking with dyslexia), each
  with three cases that vary only the inclusivity specification, each generated
  ten times independently, for sixty artifacts in total. Every artifact was
  executed, verified and photographed, then scored by a panel of seven large
  language models on both an OWASP-based security rubric and a cognitive
  accessibility rubric, three times over, giving 2,457 judgements and 36,855
  item scores. Includes the original 2025 single-run collection for reference,
  human expert evaluations, all analysis scripts, and the packaged artifacts
  used for human evaluation.
keywords:
  - large language models
  - code generation
  - software security
  - OWASP Top 10
  - accessibility
  - inclusive design
  - cognitive accessibility
  - ADHD
  - dyslexia
  - authentication
  - empirical software engineering
  - replication package
